; Turf, as the thing GroundCover scatters. Read this before turning the density
; up, because the two numbers that decide how much grass there is are not the
; two that decide what it costs.
;
; How much grass there is: per_square_metre, and nothing else. clump_count does
; not multiply it — see the note on that field — so an earlier version of this
; comment claiming "x 20 blades a clump = 220 a square metre" overstated the
; field by a factor of ten and is deleted rather than corrected.
;
; What it costs: the near clump is 80 triangles and the distant one 32, and the
; swap at distant_beyond is what buys the density. Grass is a tiny-triangle
; workload, which is the case a GPU is worst at — a quad smaller than a few
; pixels is shaded as if it were several — so the far three quarters of the
; field being a third of the geometry is worth more than the same saving
; anywhere else. draw_within is the other half of it, because cover falls off as
; the square of it.
;
; Both of those are GPU costs and they behave like it. Half again the density
; over a circle nearly two thirds wider took the drawn instances from 143k to
; 324k and cost ten frames a second of a ninety-five, while the CPU side of the
; frame did not move at all — 19.9 ms before, 20.1 ms after. Nothing here can
; make the field stutter; it can only make it slower everywhere at once.
;
; The three "where it grows" numbers are the ones that keep it off spikes and
; ledges. max_slope is the average lean of the ground; steady_over and
; steady_within are the test that a spike fails and a smooth hillside passes,
; by asking whether the four neighbours a metre and a half out are where that
; lean says they should be. steady_within has to stay above
; steady_over x tan(max_slope) = 0.61 m, or a legitimate 22-degree hillside
; fails the evenness test and the world loses every slope it has. clump_radius
; stays under steady_over for the same reason in reverse: a clump member lands
; inside the patch of ground its parent spot vouched for.

[resource]
script = ExtResource("1_species")
resource_name = "PlanetGrass"
model = ExtResource("2_model")
distant_model = ExtResource("5_far")
mesh_named = "GrassVAT"
distant_mesh_named = "GrassFar"
material = ExtResource("3_surface")
vat = ExtResource("4_vat")
distant_vat = ExtResource("6_far_vat")
height = 0.33
height_variation = 0.42
width_scale = 1.6
tilt = 11.0
above_water = 7.2
below = 5000.0
max_slope = 22.0
steady_over = 1.5
steady_within = 0.72
ground_layer = 1
minimum_claim = 0.02
maximum_arid = 1.0
maximum_frost = 1.0
terrain_tint = true
glowing_patches = 0.3
glow_patch_size = 26.0
glow_seed = 20260818
; Blades a square metre, directly. Not spots: _scatter divides the number of
; spots it tests by clump_count, so raising the clump regroups the same blades
; into fewer, bigger tufts and adds none. This is the only number that decides
; how much grass there is, and 18 was not enough of it.
per_square_metre = 28.0
clump_count = 10
clump_radius = 1.3
bare_share = 0.0
patch_size = 90.0
random_seed = 20260817
; The middle distance, which is what these five numbers are really about.
;
; Measured before touching them, both fields together put 18.4 blades a square
; metre on the ground at 20 m, 5.6 at 40, 2.4 at 50 and 1.3 at 60 — a fourteen-
; fold collapse across ground the player is looking straight at, and the reason
; the field read as a patch of turf standing in a bare plain rather than as a
; lawn going over a hill. They now run 27.9, 24.3, 15.5 and 9.0.
;
; Three of the four causes were here. Thinning began at 14 m, which is close
; enough to be inside the part of the field that is meant to look solid; it then
; had no explicit end, so it stretched to draw_within and was already most of the
; way down by 40 m; and it bottomed out at nine percent. The fourth was that the
; field simply stopped at 48 m, before the band in question had finished.
;
; thin_to is now stated rather than left to default. Leaving it unset ties where
; the thinning finishes to how far the field reaches, so every metre added to
; draw_within also slackens the near-field curve — the two want to move in
; opposite directions and cannot while they are the same number.
draw_within = 66.0
thin_from = 22.0
thin_to = 66.0
far_density = 0.24
fade_band = 0.22
shadow_within = 0.0
distant_beyond = 22.0
